    "payload": {
      "content": "When reviewing the originality of ensemble methods that modify splitting criteria (e.g., using Effort-To-Compress instead of Gini/Entropy), assess whether the new impurity measure provides a genuine conceptual advance or is a minor variation. Critically evaluate the problem formulation: if the core task remains standard classification, the motivation for the new measure (e.g., capturing order-dependence in tabular data) must be rigorously established beyond toy examples. Experimental setups are often weak if they rely solely on standard UCI datasets without introducing new benchmarks or protocols that specifically test the claimed property (e.g., order-sensitivity). Furthermore, if the proposed method underperforms standard baselines (like Random Forest), the analysis must go beyond superficial comparison to provide deep insights into why the structural impurity fails to translate into predictive power, including ablation studies or error analysis to identify conditions where the new measure is beneficial.",
      "reviewer_dimension": "ORIG",
